# Inheritance

##  What is Inheritance

Inheritance is a mechanism in Java where one class acquires the properties
and behaviors (fields and methods) of another class.



## Why use Inheritance

* Code reusability
* Reduces redundancy
* Improves maintainability
* Supports method overriding (polymorphism)



##  Basic Terminology

* Parent Class (Superclass) → class whose properties are inherited
* Child Class (Subclass) → class that inherits properties



##  Syntax

class Parent {
    // properties and methods
}

class Child extends Parent {
    // additional properties and methods
}



## Example

class Animal {
    void eat() {
        System.out.println("Animal eats");
    }
}

class Dog extends Animal {
    void bark() {
        System.out.println("Dog barks");
    }
}




##  Usage
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Dog d = new Dog();

        d.eat();   // inherited method
        d.bark();  // own method
    }
}




##  Types of Inheritance in Java

### 1. Single Inheritance

One child inherits from one parent

class A {}
class B extends A {}


### 2. Multilevel Inheritance

Chain of inheritance

class A {}
class B extends A {}
class C extends B {}


### 3. Hierarchical Inheritance

Multiple children inherit from one parent

class A {}
class B extends A {}
class C extends A {}


---

###  Multiple Inheritance (Not supported with classes)

class A {}
class B {}
class C extends A, B {}  // Not allowed


--> Java avoids ambiguity (Diamond Problem)

## super Keyword

### What it is

Used to refer to parent class members



### Call parent method

super.methodName();


###  Call parent constructor

super();



## Example with super

class Animal {
    void sound() {
        System.out.println("Animal sound");
    }
}

class Dog extends Animal {
    void sound() {
        super.sound();
        System.out.println("Dog barks");
    }
}




##  Constructor in Inheritance

* Parent constructor is called first
* If not written, Java adds `super()` automatically



##  Method Overriding in Inheritance

Child class can change parent method behavior

class Animal {
    void sound() {
        System.out.println("Animal sound");
    }
}

class Dog extends Animal {
    @Override
    void sound() {
        System.out.println("Dog barks");
    }
}

---

##  Access Modifiers & Inheritance

| Modifier  | Accessible in Child |
| --------- | ------------------- |
| public    | ✅                   |
| protected | ✅                   |
| default   | ⚠ (same package)    |
| private   | ❌                   |



##  Advantages

* Reusability of code
* Cleaner structure
* Easier maintenance
* Supports polymorphism



## Disadvantages

* Tight coupling between classes
* Can increase complexity if overused



## Best Practices

* Use inheritance only when there is "is-a" relationship

  * Dog is a Animal ✅
  * Car is a Engine ❌

* Avoid deep inheritance chains

* Prefer composition when appropriate

## Key Points

* Use `extends` keyword
* Java does not support multiple inheritance with classes
* `super` is used to access parent
* Enables method overriding
